<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Schwerin</h1><p class="st-meta">1180 CE – 1648 CE (approx.) · capital Bützow</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Mecklenburg church state founded during the conversion of the Obotrites and absorbed by the dukes of Mecklenburg after the Reformation.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The bishopric was set up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> during the conquest of the Slavic <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/obotrite-confederation">Obotrites</a>, with the Cistercian monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berno-of-schwerin">Berno</a> as its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and its seat moved to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-schwerin">Schwerin</a> around 1160. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry's</a> fall in 1180 the bishops held their estates as imperial princes, a status the dukes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-mecklenburg">Mecklenburg</a> constantly disputed.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The territory was small, consisting chiefly of lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/butzow">Bützow</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> resided, and Warin. It grew through German settlement in former Slavic lands. In the 16th century the see was held by princes of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-mecklenburg">Mecklenburg</a> house, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-duke-of-mecklenburg">Ulrich of Mecklenburg</a>, and became Lutheran, so the bishopric functioned in practice as a Mecklenburg <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 secularised it as the Principality of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-schwerin">Schwerin</a> and awarded it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg-schwerin">Duchy of Mecklenburg-Schwerin</a> as compensation for <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/swedish-wismar">Wismar</a>, which went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. It is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecklenburg-vorpommern">Mecklenburg-Vorpommern</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="prince bishopric, 1180 CE – 1648 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 82 V46 Q50 12 70 46 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 30 V82 M40 58 H60"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">prince bishopric</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.18%;width:9.31%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1180 CE – 1648 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bützow</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Berno of Schwerin, Ulrich of Mecklenburg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, then Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hochstift Schwerin</dd></dl></aside></div>
